1. Training setup model and important rules
Each candidate must create and use their own AWS account. Inside that account, each candidate will create one IAM user with the AWS-managed AdministratorAccess policy, enable console access, and create an access key for AWS CLI exercises. Do not use an IAM Identity Center user supplied by the trainer.
☐ Create an individual AWS account that you own and are authorized to use. Do not use an employer's or another person's account.
☐ The account root user is for account setup and tasks that require root. Use the IAM admin user for normal training tasks.
☐ Enable MFA on both the root user and IAM admin user wherever supported. Never create root access keys.
☐ AdministratorAccess is broad: it can create, modify, and delete most resources and may cause charges. Follow the trainer's service and spending guidance.
☐ Never share passwords, secret access keys, MFA codes, recovery codes, or API keys. Never commit secrets to GitHub.
☐ AWS console labels and signup flows may differ. If the AWS signup flow creates a simplified project-only experience that does not support IAM console users, use the full/advanced AWS account signup experience or follow AWS's current official guidance for enabling it.  
AWS account signup options: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/getting-started.html
AWS IAM user creation guidance: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_users_create.html
2. Laptop, browser, and internet
Purpose: all setup and practical exercises run from your laptop.
Use a laptop where you can install applications, or arrange installation permission before training.
Update your operating system and install a current browser such as Chrome, Edge, Firefox, or Safari.
Check that your internet connection can open HTTPS websites and download installers.
Keep approximately 10 GB of free disk space available for developer tools, Python packages, and project files.
Verification test: open the official GitHub, AWS, Postman, Pinecone, Python, and VS Code websites in your browser.
☐ ☐ Browser and internet work
☐ ☐ Software installation permissions confirmed
☐ ☐ Free disk space checked
3. Create your AWS account
Purpose: your personal AWS training account is where you will create and test cloud resources. You are responsible for monitoring its usage and charges.
Open the official AWS account signup page and choose the full/advanced signup path if offered; the course requires classic IAM user console access and AWS services used in the training.
Enter an email address you control. Verify the email using the code sent by AWS.
Create a strong, unique root-user password and store it securely.
Enter the requested account/contact details and complete phone or identity verification.
Complete billing/payment details if AWS requires them for your signup path. Review the plan and any Free Tier or credit terms shown during signup.
Wait for AWS to confirm account activation, then sign in as the root user once to finish security setup.
Verification test: you can sign in to the AWS Management Console as the root user and open the console home page.
AWS account creation and activation: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/getting-started.html
AWS account creation page: https://aws.amazon.com/resources/create-account/
4. Secure the root user and configure a budget
Purpose: root access can control the entire account. MFA and budget alerts reduce avoidable risk, but budget alerts are not a hard spending cap.
4A. Enable root-user MFA
Sign in to the AWS console using the root email address.
Open the account menu and choose Security credentials (or the current equivalent).
Find Multi-factor authentication (MFA) and choose Assign MFA device.
Follow the wizard to configure an authenticator app or another supported MFA device.
Sign out and sign back in to verify the MFA challenge works. Store recovery information securely.
Test: root sign-in requires the configured MFA method.
Root-user security best practices: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/root-user-best-practices.html
4B. Create a monthly AWS budget
Open Billing and Cost Management in the AWS console.
Open Budgets and choose Create budget.
Choose a monthly cost budget or the closest available cost-budget template.
Name it (for example, AI-Training-Monthly-Budget) and set a low amount you can afford. If unsure, ask the trainer for the suggested amount before creating resources.
Configure email alerts at suitable thresholds, such as 50%, 80%, and 100%, if available. Use an email address you check regularly.
Create the budget and confirm it appears in the Budgets list.
Verification test: the budget is visible and notification recipients are correct. Remember: AWS Budgets sends alerts; it does not automatically stop resources or guarantee a spending limit.
AWS Budgets guide: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/budgets-managing-costs.html
5. Create one IAM admin user with console password
Purpose: use a separate IAM identity for day-to-day exercises instead of signing in as root. The user requested for this training must have AdministratorAccess within the candidate's own training account.
Important: AWS recommends temporary/federated credentials for many human-user scenarios. This training checklist follows the specified IAM-user lab model. Use it only in your own training account and protect it carefully.
Sign in to the AWS console as the root user for initial setup, then open the IAM service.
In the IAM navigation pane, choose Users, then Create user.
Enter a clear username, for example ai-training-admin. Do not use another candidate's credentials.
Enable the option to provide user access to the AWS Management Console. Select the IAM user option if the wizard asks which type of user to create.
Set a strong, unique console password. If AWS offers a change-password-at-first-sign-in option, enable it unless the training instructions require otherwise.
On the permissions step, choose Attach policies directly and search for the AWS-managed policy named AdministratorAccess. Select it.
Review the permissions and username, then create the user.
Save the IAM sign-in URL/account sign-in details and username in a secure password manager. If AWS displays a one-time password or download file, store it securely and do not email or share it.
Verification test: sign out of root and sign in through the IAM user sign-in URL using the IAM username and console password. Complete any password-change or MFA setup prompts.
Security note: do not attach AdministratorAccess to the root user (root already has full access). Attach it to the new IAM user only.
Create an IAM user in your AWS account: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_users_create.html
AWS-managed AdministratorAccess policy: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-managed-policy/latest/reference/AdministratorAccess.html
6. Enable MFA for the IAM admin user
Purpose: MFA adds a second verification step if the password is compromised.
Sign in as the new IAM admin user.
Open the account/security credentials area and locate MFA.
Choose Assign MFA device and follow the on-screen steps for an authenticator app or another supported device.
Sign out and sign back in to test MFA.
Verification test: console sign-in requires the IAM username/password and the configured MFA step.
IAM user MFA instructions: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_mfa_enable_virtual.html
7. Create an access key for AWS CLI
Purpose: the AWS CLI uses programmatic credentials to call AWS APIs from your laptop. The access key consists of an Access key ID and a Secret access key.
Sign in as the IAM admin user and open IAM → Users → your ai-training-admin user.
Open Security credentials, find Access keys, and choose Create access key.
Choose Command Line Interface (CLI) as the use case if the wizard offers that choice. Review the warning and acknowledge the intended training use.
Create the access key. Copy the Access key ID and Secret access key into a secure password manager or the approved local credential setup. The secret is shown only at creation time.
Do not paste the key into a Python file, terminal command, shared document, email, chat, screenshot, or GitHub repository.
If you lose or expose the secret, deactivate/delete that key and create a replacement. Do not create multiple keys unless there is a clear need.
Verification test: the key is created and shown as active in the user's Security credentials page. The next section tests whether the CLI can use it.
Important: long-lived access keys are sensitive credentials. For this course, use them only for the required lab and remove them when no longer needed.
AWS IAM access-key best practices: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/securing_access-keys.html
Create access keys for an IAM user: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_access-keys.html
8. Install AWS CLI v2 and configure credentials
Purpose: AWS CLI allows you to verify identity and run AWS commands without relying only on the browser.
Download and install AWS CLI version 2 for your operating system from the official AWS instructions.
Close and reopen your terminal (Windows: Git Bash or PowerShell; macOS/Linux: Terminal).
Check the installation by running the command below.
Configure a named profile called training. Enter the IAM user's Access key ID and Secret access key only when prompted interactively. Do not put secrets in the command itself.
Set the default region to the one specified by the trainer (for example, ap-south-1 only if the trainer selects Mumbai) and choose json as the output format.
aws --version
aws configure --profile training
Then verify identity:
aws sts get-caller-identity --profile training
Verification test: aws --version prints AWS CLI v2; get-caller-identity returns JSON containing Account and Arn. Confirm the account ID is your own account and the ARN names your IAM user. Do not post this output publicly.
AWS CLI installation: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html
AWS CLI configuration: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html
9. Install Git and Git Bash / Terminal
Purpose: Git records source-code changes. Git Bash provides a Unix-like command-line shell on Windows.
Windows: download and install Git for Windows; Git Bash is included.
macOS: install Git through Xcode Command Line Tools or the official Git distribution.
Linux: install Git using the operating system's package manager.
Open Git Bash or Terminal and set the name and email that should appear on your commits.
git --version
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-verified-email@example.com"
Verification test: git --version prints a version and the configuration commands complete without error.
Git downloads: https://git-scm.com/downloads
Set up Git: https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/git-basics/set-up-git
10. Create a GitHub account and test Git push
Purpose: GitHub hosts the training repository and lets you submit or share code. Keep the repository private unless the trainer requests a public one.
Create a GitHub account and verify the email address.
Enable two-factor authentication (2FA).
Create a repository named ai-training-practice. Do not initialize it with files if you plan to use the exact first-push test below, or clone the repository if it already contains files.
Use GitHub's browser-based sign-in or supported credential manager when Git prompts for authentication. Your GitHub account password is not normally accepted as a Git HTTPS password.
If you created an empty repository, run the following commands in Git Bash/Terminal:
mkdir ai-training-practice
cd ai-training-practice
echo "AI training setup complete" > readiness.txt
git init
git add readiness.txt
git commit -m "Add readiness test"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/ai-training-practice.git
git push -u origin main
Replace YOUR-USERNAME with your GitHub username. If you already cloned the repository, do not run git init or add the remote again; create a file, commit, and push from the cloned folder.
Verification test: readiness.txt appears in the repository on GitHub.
Create a GitHub account: https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/start-your-journey/creating-an-account-on-github
GitHub 2FA: https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/securing-your-account-with-two-factor-authentication-2fa
